  "followNote": "15/10/2026 (look review v8): the sidebar newsletter panel (.je-side--news, the accent with its own words) carries a consent line and a link written white: 2.26:1 on Moxie’s light accent; every word in the panel takes the accent’s own text. The current page's number keeps its accent fill and takes the text the system sets on its accent (accent-on): the rule for the other numbers had painted it the page text, so under Toggl Track it was pink on its own pink fill (1:1) and under Moxie light on light blue (1.96:1; re-measured 12/10/2026 with every heading and text judged). theme rules that paint literal colours the tokenizer left (wp-ja-essence.css: .je-pagination__link and __counter #000 on a 10% black chip, the cards' Read more #252424): under a dark system they stayed black on the dark page and cards (Moxie 1.00-1.12, Toggl Track 1.00-1.26, re-measured 11/10/2026); they take the system's text colour. 14/10/2026 (#1315, two modes): the category chip's link (.je-badge a) took the theme's own dark rule (:root[data-theme=dark] .je-lead__card .je-badge a, a light chip) once the dark side was shown, the system's light text on it at 1.06:1 under Spotify; it takes the page colour and the heading text, as the theme's light rule paints it 14/10/2026 (final measure): the footer's note under its button (je-detail.css .je-footer__small: light-dark(rgba(37, 36, 36, 0.7), #8c97a8), the theme's own text and dark muted written as literals) kept #8c97a8 on a system's dark side, 4.46:1 on Pitch's derived dark footer; it takes the system's text colour, as the theme's light half paints it 14/10/2026 (look review of the final measure): the category badge rule painted the post-terms WRAPPER (.je-badge, display:block) with the surface, a full-width bar across every hero card under all fifteen systems; the chip is .je-badge a, which has its own rule — the wrapper keeps no ground. The drawer toggle's three bars are spans in currentColor over a transparent button: the toggle takes the system's text as its colour, never as a fill (a fill drew a solid disc that hid the bars).",
